One JSON-schema validator per process instead of jsonschema.validate() per record

What: TransformResources builds the validator for the warehouse's schema once (validator_for,
check_schema), and transform_batch_impl validates each record with it (best_match of
iter_errors), instead of calling jsonschema.validate(instance, schema) for every record.

Why: validate() checks the schema against its metaschema and builds a validator on every call.
With the warehouse's schema that is about 21 ms per record against 0.18 ms for the record itself
(modama staging finding F16), about 60 % of a batch of 250 records (perf/phase-timer.py: 5.2 s
of the batch); a batch of 250 ran 2.5 times as fast with it in the modama search stack's VM.

Same result: in jsonschema 4.26.0 (the transform venv's), validate() is exactly
`cls = validator_for(schema); cls.check_schema(schema); error = best_match(cls(schema).iter_errors(
instance)); if error is not None: raise error`. The schema is checked once, at start, instead of
per record, so an invalid schema now fails TransformResources() rather than every record.

Against metadata-warehouse v1.10.0; applied with `patch -p1` in the image build (modama search
stack, warehouse-airflow.Dockerfile; @weber, decision 177, change cpu-budget D9).

